From: evidence-hooks-next (the WP-1 H-3 instrument; cure D9, since superseded for the work-tree gate)
Subject: [PATCH] clean-tree gates: A-126 judges the porcelain against the mission's DECLARED admitted set; the D9 name-pattern exemption remains in the spec-accept gate

REGENERATED MECHANICALLY. The diff body below is the output of

    git diff <pre-exemption commit> HEAD -- tools/mission-lib.ps1 tools/mission-spec-accept.ps1

stored as git prints it, LF line endings and no CR byte. It is not hand-maintained, and
nothing in it is hand-edited: hand-maintenance is what once produced a defect (both @@
new-side counts understated by 2, a patch that READ correct and did not APPLY).

WHAT THIS PATCH IS FOR. Design v2.3 SS4.6 records the defect the first version of this file cured,
measured on the live harness:

  "Clean-tree preconditions become unsatisfiable by construction while hooks are live.
   Assert-CleanGitTree exempts `?? .claude/` and `?? .mission-runtime/` by name; the A-126 gate
   Assert-CleanWorkingTree exempts nothing without -AllowPaths. `.mission/evidence/` is exempt in
   neither, and it REGENERATES -- a new session-<id>/ per session, created by the act of running
   one -- so relocate-and-restore is defeated in kind: the path's name is not known in advance and
   reappears mid-run. Framework ceremonies using -NoCommit plus explicit-path commits are
   unaffected; mission lifecycle ceremonies (spec-accept, promote-contract, reopen, close) are
   BLOCKED UNCONDITIONALLY."

SS17.3 carries that as a standing operational constraint. This patch retires it. What has changed
since the first fix is HOW the two gates retire it, and the two answers are now genuinely different.
That divergence is the first thing a reader of this file has to be told, because the file looks
lopsided until it is:

THE TWO HALVES NO LONGER SHARE A MECHANISM.

  tools/mission-lib.ps1, Assert-CleanWorkingTree -- CURED. The admitted set is no longer a code
  constant. The gate dot-sources
  tools/lam/lam-verify-admission.ps1 -- the SAME library the advance-admission gate reads the
  declaration with, rather than a second dialect of it -- and judges every porcelain line against
  `admitted_porcelain` in the mission's own .mission/mission.yaml, via
  Get-LamDeclaredAdmittedPorcelain and Get-LamPorcelainState. The match is over the WHOLE porcelain
  line, STATUS CODE INCLUDED, so a declared prefix appearing at a status other than `?? ` is still
  judged. The degradations all REFUSE: a failing `git status`, an unreadable or malformed
  declaration, and -- for a mission that declares nothing -- an admitted set that admits nothing.
  The two collapsed-form code exemptions `'^\.mission/evidence/'` and `'^\.mission/gates/'` are
  GONE from this gate: they matched the path only, after the XY status code had been stripped, so
  they tolerated a MODIFIED or DELETED tracked evidence file exactly as they tolerated an untracked
  store, they tolerated the whole evidence plane rather than the declared `verify-` prefix, and
  being CODE no mission could narrow them. The D9 `session-` name-pattern exemption is dropped with
  them, deliberately: its premise -- that no CALLER can name a session id in advance -- is a fact
  about -AllowPaths, and a DECLARED prefix `"?? .mission/evidence/session-"` satisfies that premise
  without reinstating an unnarrowable code constant. The price is stated in the source: if a
  `session-<id>` directory reappears, every lifecycle ceremony refuses until the operator adds that
  one entry to admitted_porcelain -- and the refusal names both the offending lines and the file
  the set is declared in, so the block is self-curing rather than mysterious.

  tools/mission-spec-accept.ps1, Assert-CleanGitTree -- NOT CURED. It still carries the D9 and
  first-fix forms: the session-level anchor `'^\?\? \.mission/evidence/session-'` kept for intent,
  and the two collapsed-form prefixes `'^\?\? \.mission/evidence/'` and `'^\?\? \.mission/gates/'`
  that are what actually fire, because git collapses a wholly-untracked directory to its shallowest
  path and the session anchor therefore matches NOTHING on a fresh install. This half is a SIBLING
  OF THE DEFECT the cure removed from the other gate, and it now DIVERGES from it. It was named
  rather than fixed: tools/mission-spec-accept.ps1 was outside the declared file set of the change
  that cured Assert-CleanWorkingTree, and it is outside this one's too. The divergence is recorded
  as an open residual, not as something claimed. It is carried here, visibly,
  rather than quietly made to look symmetric -- WP-1 H-3 asserts each half's own mechanism
  separately for exactly that reason.

WHY THE SESSION-LEVEL PATTERN ALONE WAS NEVER ENOUGH, and
why the spec-accept half still carries three patterns rather than one. Anchoring at
`.mission/evidence/session-` was correct in intent and silently inert in fact, for two independent
reasons, both DRIVEN on the live smoke harness rather than reasoned:

  1. GIT COLLAPSES A WHOLLY-UNTRACKED DIRECTORY to its shallowest path. With nothing under
     `.mission/evidence/` tracked, `git status --porcelain` emits ONE line -- `?? .mission/evidence/`
     -- and never the per-session lines this pattern was written for. So the exemption matched
     nothing and every ceremony was blocked exactly as SS17.3 describes. The pattern encoded an
     UNSTATED DEPENDENCY on H-3's committed-evidence policy having already run once; a fresh install
     is precisely the case where it has not.
  2. Even once the plane HAS tracked content, `verify-<id>/` self-capture directories sit beside the
     session ones and do not match `session-` either.

E24, the rule this cost us: EVERY PORCELAIN PATTERN IS VALIDATED AGAINST A LIVE PORCELAIN LINE AT
AUTHORING TIME. A pattern matching nothing at authoring is a build error, not a silent no-op. The
work-tree cure is that rule applied one level up: the patterns now come from the mission that owns the
residue, so they are validated where the residue actually is.

COMPENSATING CONTROL, corrected. The sentence that stood here said both directories are
agent-unwritable, "evidence guard; T-3". That named the wrong tier for one of them, and the
source now says it right. `.mission/evidence` IS on the guard's protected-path tier, denied for
every role, reads included. `.mission/gates` IS NOT on that tier: it is reached by a text-match
predicate on the file-tool and shell routes, by the act-match tripwire on
tools/lam/mission-gate.ps1 in executable position, and by an `Edit(.mission/gates/**)` deny in two
of the three role templates -- all four of them TRIPWIRES, not boundaries. The
mission-lib half no longer needs that control at all, because it no longer tolerates either
directory by code. The spec-accept half still does, and still rests on it.

WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not a claim that committed evidence is safe to commit. The committed-
evidence policy is separate and narrower (LAM-ACCEPT H-3: manifest + digests only, committed by
ceremony, and only when capture_gaps is empty). This patch changes a tree-clean precondition; it
grants nothing about what is committed.

DIRECTIONALITY, which WP-1 H-3 and H-3b measure rather than assume. The pre-image side of this diff
is <project> as shipped WITHOUT the exemption (tools/ as shipped before it); the post-image side is the
cured tools/ this diff was regenerated against, identified by the post-image blob ids on its two
`index` lines. SUPERSEDED: WP-1 used to measure this patch
against real copies of the LIVE tools/, where it reverse-applied and failed forward, so every
lifecycle edit to either file turned WP-1 red by construction. It now measures the patch against
the pre-image it was cut from, pinned below and stored beside this file. Against that pre-image the
patch must apply FORWARD and must NOT reverse-apply -- one fixed direction, since the subject tree
is no longer ambiguous -- and the tree the forward apply derives must carry exactly the post-image
ids on the `index` lines. An EMPTY patch fails that rule either way: this git refuses empty input
at parse time, and when told to accept it (the allow-empty option) an empty patch applies in BOTH
directions, which the reverse leg refuses.

PRE-IMAGE PINS. One block per file of the diff below. `fixture` is the file WP-1 H-3 copies into its
scratch tree under the path the block names; `blob` is git's object id for that fixture's content;
`lf-bytes` and `lf-sha256` are measured over its LF bytes (every CRLF pair collapsed to LF), which is
also the content the blob id is computed over. WP-1 H-3 reads these figures from this face and
re-measures them on every run, and a fixture, a pin or an `index` line that disagrees is red. The
LIVE tools/ gates are judged separately, by behaviour, in WP-1 H-16. One disclosed edit: the spec-accept
fixture differs from the pre-exemption tools/ in one message string (its line 178, the host project's name
made generic, outside every hunk), so its pin and both ids on its `index` line were re-derived with git.
A second disclosed edit: a person's first name in three of its lines (30, 374 and 392, two
comments and one message string, outside every hunk) reads `client`, as in tools/; its pin and both ids
were re-derived again, the patch applying forward to it and refused in reverse. A third disclosed edit: the mission-lib fixture's line 93 (a comment, outside every hunk) names the corpus oracle's bump commit rather than its id, as in tools/; its pin and both ids on its `index` line were re-derived with git. A fourth disclosed edit: the mission-lib fixture's witnessed-substrate check (outside every hunk) requires the declared terminal activity and has no built-in default, and its comments name no record of the host project, as in tools/; its pin and both ids on its `index` line were re-derived with git, the patch applying forward to it and refused in reverse. A fifth disclosed edit: the mission-lib fixture's two witnessed-substrate comments (outside every hunk) describe the upstream guard and the event structure without naming the host project's client or engine, and the spec-accept fixture's -RunbookValidated message (outside every hunk) names no host container, as in tools/; both pins and all four ids on their `index` lines were re-derived with git, the patch applying forward to them and refused in reverse.

